\chapter{The command-line interface}

The official Zulip CLI tools---[[zulip-api]] and [[zulip-send]]---cover
message sending and a handful of administrative operations, but lack
basic discovery commands.
When we need to send a direct message with [[zulip-send]], we must
already know the recipient's email address; there is no built-in way to
look it up.
Similarly, there is no command to list the available streams (channels),
inspect the topics inside a stream, or read a stream's history from the
terminal.
Nor is there any way to find a message whose location we have
forgotten---the question that matters most once a realm has enough
history to be worth searching, and the one an automated agent has
before it has any other.

The [[zulipcli]] command fills these gaps.
User administration---listing, inviting, and inspecting---is grouped
under a single [[users]] command family, while the remaining verbs
stay flat because each speaks to a different Zulip concept:
\begin{description}
\item[users list] List users on the server, optionally filtered by a
  regex matched against name, Zulip email, and real (delivery) email.
\item[users invited] List pending (unaccepted) invitations.
\item[users invite] Invite one or more new users by email.
\item[send] Send a message to a stream or directly to one or more users.
\item[dm] Read direct-message history, optionally for one conversation.
\item[streams] List available streams.
\item[topics] List the topics inside a stream.
\item[read] Read the history of a stream or one topic within it.
\item[search] Find messages anywhere in the history, one compact line
  per hit, optionally limited to given streams, topics, or a sender.
\item[terms] Look up the vocabulary this realm actually uses, from an
  index built up while reading messages.
\item[bot] Run an external worker command, given after [[--]], whenever
  unread messages exist, feeding the unread messages to it on stdin.
\end{description}

We keep the subcommand pattern familiar from Git and the
[[canvaslms]] tool, but implement it with [[Typer]] instead of
[[argparse]]. Typer still lets each subcommand read like an ordinary
Python function, while adding shell completion for command names,
option names, and argument values.


\section{Program structure}

The term index of \cref{sec:term-index} is the only part of this module
that needs storage and fuzzy matching, and both come from the standard
library: [[sqlite3]] for the index itself, [[difflib]] for term
similarity, and [[os]] for the [[XDG_DATA_HOME]] lookup.
Keeping them stdlib matters because this package declares exactly three
runtime dependencies---[[zulip]], [[typer]], and [[rich]]---and a
vocabulary cache is not worth a fourth.

