\section{Argument parsing}

The module still exposes a standalone [[app]] whose callback accepts
\texttt{-{}-zuliprc} to override the default configuration file path.
That callback stores the resolved path in the root context, so each
subcommand can ask one helper for a configured client.
Typing the option as a [[Path]] also gives us filesystem completion for
free.

We also expose [[build_embedded_app()]] for host applications that want
to mount the same command tree under their own subcommand name.
The embedded form declares no root options, so the host can choose the
mount name with [[add_typer()]] and populate [[ctx.obj["zuliprc"]]] in
its own callback.
The standalone form keeps Typer's completion-installation flags, while
the embedded form disables them by default so the host owns that
surface.

<<constants>>=
APP_HELP = "Command-line interface for Zulip"
@

<<functions>>=
app = typer.Typer(
    help=APP_HELP,
    add_completion=True,
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
@

<<functions>>=
@app.callback()
def configure_app(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    zuliprc: Annotated[
        Optional[Path],
        typer.Option(
            help="path to zuliprc config file (default: ~/.zuliprc)",
            dir_okay=False,
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Configure shared CLI options."""
    ctx.ensure_object(dict)
    if zuliprc is not None:
        ctx.obj["zuliprc"] = str(zuliprc)
    else:
        ctx.obj.setdefault("zuliprc", None)
@

<<functions>>=
def _get_root_zuliprc(ctx: typer.Context):
    """Return the zuliprc override stored in the root context."""
    root_ctx = ctx.find_root()
    obj = getattr(root_ctx, "obj", None) or {}
    if "zuliprc" in obj:
        return obj["zuliprc"]
    params = getattr(root_ctx, "params", None) or {}
    return params.get("zuliprc")
@

Client construction can fail before any API call if the configured
[[zuliprc]] path is missing or malformed.
Those failures are still ordinary command-line errors, so we report them
through the same [[Error: ...]] path instead of showing a traceback.

<<functions>>=
def _get_client_from_context(ctx):
    """Create a Zulip client from the current Typer context."""
    try:
        return zulipcli.get_client(_get_root_zuliprc(ctx))
    except (configparser.Error, zulip.ZulipError) as error:
        _exit_with_error(str(error))
@

The standalone and embedded applications share the same command
handlers.
We therefore build the embedded Typer application as a fresh object,
normalise its context in a minimal callback, and register the shared
handlers onto it.
That keeps the standalone and embedded command surfaces aligned without
forcing either form to own the other's root options.

<<functions>>=
def build_embedded_app(
    *,
    help: Optional[str] = None,
    add_completion: bool = False,
    no_args_is_help: bool = True,
) -> typer.Typer:
    """Return a Typer app suitable for mounting in another Typer app.

    Example:
        parent = typer.Typer()
        parent.add_typer(build_embedded_app(), name="zulip")

    The host callback should store the resolved zuliprc path in
    `ctx.obj["zuliprc"]` before the embedded commands run.
    """
    embedded_app = typer.Typer(
        help=APP_HELP if help is None else help,
        add_completion=add_completion,
        no_args_is_help=no_args_is_help,
    )

    @embedded_app.callback()
    def configure_embedded_app(ctx: typer.Context):
        """Prepare shared CLI state for embedded use."""
        ctx.ensure_object(dict)
        ctx.obj.setdefault("zuliprc", None)

    _register_commands(embedded_app)
    return embedded_app
@


\section{Reporting Zulip errors}

The ordinary commands should all report user-facing Zulip failures in
the same way: print the message on stderr and exit with status [[1]].
Factoring that policy into two helpers keeps each command focused on its
own data handling instead of repeating the same error-checking branch.

The completion callbacks are the exception. They must stay silent, so
they continue to inspect the raw Zulip result directly and return an
empty candidate list on failure.

<<functions>>=
def _exit_with_error(message):
    """Print an error message and exit with status 1."""
    print(f"Error: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(1)
@

<<functions>>=
def _require_success(result):
    """Return a successful Zulip API result or exit with its error."""
    if result.get("result") != "success":
        _exit_with_error(result.get("msg", "unknown Zulip API error"))
    return result
@


\section{Completion lookups}
\label{sec:completion}

Typer already handles command-name and option-name completion.
The remaining work is value completion for live Zulip data.
These callbacks must stay silent on errors because shells treat stdout
as completion output.
If the client cannot be created or a Zulip API request fails, we simply
return no candidates.

Typer also gives completion callbacks the parsed command context.
That is enough for this CLI: the root context carries the resolved
[[zuliprc]] path, whether it came from the standalone [[--zuliprc]]
option or a host application's callback, and the active command context
already contains the selected stream for [[read TOPIC]] and
[[send --topic TOPIC]].

<<functions>>=
def _get_completion_client(ctx: typer.Context):
    """Create a Zulip client for shell completion lookups."""
    try:
        return zulipcli.get_client(_get_root_zuliprc(ctx))
    except Exception:
        return None
@

<<functions>>=
def _matches_completion(candidate: str, incomplete: str):
    """Return whether candidate matches the current shell fragment."""
    if not incomplete:
        return True
    return candidate.casefold().startswith(incomplete.casefold())
@

<<functions>>=
def complete_stream_names(ctx: typer.Context, incomplete: str):
    """Autocomplete stream names."""
    client = _get_completion_client(ctx)
    if client is None:
        return []

    result = client.get_streams()
    if result.get("result") != "success":
        return []

    return [
        stream["name"]
        for stream in sorted(
            result["streams"],
            key=lambda stream: stream["name"].casefold(),
        )
        if _matches_completion(stream["name"], incomplete)
    ]
@

Topics depend on the selected stream, so their completion callback first
resolves that stream name and then asks Zulip for the available topics.
That same callback serves [[read STREAM TOPIC]],
[[send --stream STREAM --topic TOPIC]], and
[[search --stream STREAM --topic TOPIC]].

That last caller is why the selected stream needs normalising before we
use it.
The [[search]] command accepts [[--stream]] more than once, and Click
collects a repeatable option into a tuple rather than a string.
Passing that tuple on to [[get_stream_id()]] would ask Zulip to resolve
a stream whose name is the printed form of a Python tuple, and the
lookup would simply fail.
We keep the last value because the shell is completing the topic that
follows the most recently typed [[--stream]], so that is the stream the
user currently has in mind.

<<functions>>=
def _selected_stream_name(ctx: typer.Context):
    """Return the stream selected in the current command context."""
    stream = ctx.params.get("stream")
    if isinstance(stream, (list, tuple)):
        return stream[-1] if stream else None
    return stream
@

<<functions>>=
def complete_topic_names(ctx: typer.Context, incomplete: str):
    """Autocomplete topic names for the selected stream."""
    stream = _selected_stream_name(ctx)
    if not stream:
        return []

    client = _get_completion_client(ctx)
    if client is None:
        return []

    result = client.get_stream_id(stream)
    if result.get("result") != "success":
        return []

    result = client.get_stream_topics(result["stream_id"])
    if result.get("result") != "success":
        return []

    return [
        topic["name"]
        for topic in sorted(
            result["topics"],
            key=lambda topic: topic["name"].casefold(),
        )
        if _matches_completion(topic["name"], incomplete)
    ]
@

