Direct-message completion must cope with the fact that shells differ
in how they render help text.
Zsh (with descriptions) and fish display the second element of each
[[(value, help)]] tuple next to the value; bash shows only the value
and filters it against whatever the user has typed so far.
A completion that returns [[(email, full_name)]] is therefore useless
on bash when the user is typing a name: no email starts with
\enquote{Dan}, so bash offers nothing---even though [[full_name]]
would match.

The fix is to emit one candidate per \emph{matching field} rather than
one per user.
If the user's prefix matches the full name, we return the full name as
a value; if it matches the Zulip email, we return the email; if it
matches the delivery (real) email, we return that.
This works end-to-end because [[send --to]] is a regex: any of the
three values is a valid input that resolves back to the same user.
A user whose name, Zulip email, and delivery email all share the
same prefix therefore appears up to three times---one candidate per
field---which is what bash needs to actually show the name.

When two of a member's fields collide (bots often have
[[email == delivery_email]], for instance), emitting the same
\emph{value} twice buys nothing: the shell inserts the same string
either way, help text is attached to whichever row the shell picks,
and the duplicate is just visual noise.
We therefore track the values already emitted and skip repeats.

<<functions>>=
def complete_user_emails(ctx: typer.Context, incomplete: str):
    """Autocomplete direct-message recipients."""
    client = _get_completion_client(ctx)
    if client is None:
        return []

    result = client.get_users()
    if result.get("result") != "success":
        return []

    completions = []
    seen_values = set()
    for member in sorted(
        result["members"],
        key=lambda member: member["full_name"].casefold(),
    ):
        if not member.get("is_active", True):
            continue

        email = member["email"]
        full_name = member["full_name"]
        delivery_email = member.get("delivery_email") or ""

        for value, help_text in (
            (full_name, email),
            (email, full_name),
            (delivery_email, full_name),
        ):
            if not value or value in seen_values:
                continue
            if not _matches_completion(value, incomplete):
                continue
            completions.append((value, help_text))
            seen_values.add(value)
    return completions
@

