\section{The \texttt{users} subcommand group}
\label{sec:users}

User administration is the primary motivation for this tool, and a
typical workflow chains three related questions: who is on the
server, who has been invited but not yet joined, and how do I invite
someone new?
Grouping those verbs under a single [[users]] command family keeps
the top-level namespace tidy and makes the relationship obvious from
the help output.
The flat verbs that follow ([[send]], [[dm]], [[streams]], [[topics]],
[[read]], [[bot]]) each speak to a different Zulip concept and do not
gain anything from further nesting.

We declare the group up-front so each per-verb handler below can
attach itself via [[@users_app.command(...)]] as it is introduced.
Setting [[no_args_is_help=True]] makes [[zulipcli users]] print the
subcommand list rather than failing silently---useful both for
discovery and as a migration signal for anyone still typing the flat
[[zulipcli users PATTERN]] form that this tool used to accept.

<<functions>>=
users_app = typer.Typer(
    help="List, invite, and inspect users on the server.",
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
@

Zulip's invitation API identifies roles by integer codes, and the
same codes come back when we list pending invitations.
Defining the label$\leftrightarrow$code mapping once, next to the
commands that consume it, means the rest of the code never has to
touch the raw integers---and the two tables stay in sync because the
reverse lookup is derived from the forward one.

