\subsection{Listing users: \texttt{users list}}

The Zulip API's [[get_users]] method returns every user with fields
like [[full_name]], [[email]], [[delivery_email]], [[user_id]],
[[is_bot]], and [[is_active]].
The library also exposes a [[get_members]] alias that simply forwards
to [[get_users]]; it is kept around for backwards compatibility, and
its docstring already warns that a deprecation warning may be added
later.
We call the canonical [[get_users]] so the code keeps working when
that warning lands.

The response shape still uses [[members]] as its top-level key,
because the server's JSON format predates the client-side rename and
cannot change without breaking every existing client.
That is why the rest of this file reads [[result["members"]]] even
though the call is [[get_users]].

The [[email]] field is the address Zulip uses for API routing
(often [[user42@subdomain.zulipchat.com]] when the realm hides real
addresses), whereas [[delivery_email]] is the mailbox an operator
actually wants to reach.
When the caller is an organisation admin and the realm's policy
permits it, [[delivery_email]] carries the user's real email;
otherwise Zulip returns [[None]].

Showing both columns lets scripts correlate a Zulip identity with a
real inbox in one shot, which is the whole reason this command
exists.
We print tab-separated lines so [[grep]], [[cut]], and [[sort]]
continue to work, and we print an empty field when Zulip withheld
[[delivery_email]]---that keeps the column count stable for
[[cut -f3]] consumers.

Several commands in this file accept several regexes with the same
semantics: compile them case-insensitively, OR them together, and emit
each matching row only once.
We centralise that policy here so [[users list]], [[send]], [[dm]], and
[[streams]] all agree on what it means to match a set of regexes.

<<functions>>=
def _list_pattern_strs(pattern_strs):
    """Return zero or more pattern strings as a list."""
    if pattern_strs is None:
        return []
    if isinstance(pattern_strs, str):
        return [pattern_strs]
    return list(pattern_strs)
@

<<functions>>=
def _compile_patterns(pattern_strs):
    """Compile pattern strings as case-insensitive regexes."""
    return [
        re.compile(pattern_str, re.IGNORECASE)
        for pattern_str in _list_pattern_strs(pattern_strs)
    ]
@

<<functions>>=
def _describe_patterns(pattern_strs):
    """Return a human-readable description of pattern strings."""
    pattern_strs = _list_pattern_strs(pattern_strs)
    if len(pattern_strs) == 1:
        return f"pattern {pattern_strs[0]!r}"
    return f"patterns {pattern_strs!r}"
@

<<functions>>=
def _matches_patterns(values, patterns):
    """Return whether any value matches any compiled pattern."""
    if not patterns:
        return True
    return any(
        pattern.search(value)
        for pattern in patterns
        for value in values
        if value
    )
@

The member resolver applies the bot and activation filters before the
regex checks, because those boolean tests are cheaper than a regex
search and because every caller wants repeated or overlapping regexes to
collapse to one row per matched user.

<<functions>>=
def _resolve_members_matching_patterns(
    client,
    pattern_strs,
    *,
    include_bots,
    include_deactivated,
):
    """Return users matching the given patterns and account filters."""
    result = _require_success(client.get_users())
    patterns = _compile_patterns(pattern_strs)
    matches = []
    for member in sorted(result["members"],
                         key=lambda m: m["full_name"]):
        if not include_bots and member.get("is_bot", False):
            continue
        if not include_deactivated \
                and not member.get("is_active", True):
            continue
        haystacks = [
            member["full_name"],
            member["email"],
            member.get("delivery_email") or "",
        ]
        if not _matches_patterns(haystacks, patterns):
            continue
        matches.append(member)
    return matches
