\subsection{Inviting users: \texttt{users invite}}

Creating an invitation is a [[POST]] to the same [[invites]]
endpoint.
The API requires at least one invitee email; the rest---role,
expiration, and pre-subscribed streams---have server-side defaults
that most callers accept.
We expose the full option set from day one so an operator onboarding
a cohort can set role and starting streams in a single call rather
than accepting a default and editing it afterwards through the web
UI.

Role is exposed as a string label rather than the raw integer code,
both because the labels are easier to remember and because Typer
turns an [[Enum]] subclass into shell-completion choices
automatically---no custom completion helper needed.

<<functions>>=
class InviteRole(str, Enum):
    """Labels for Zulip's numeric organisation-role codes."""

    owner = "owner"
    admin = "admin"
    moderator = "moderator"
    member = "member"
    guest = "guest"
@

The option name [[--as]] reads naturally (\enquote{invite Alice as
admin}) but [[as]] is a Python keyword, so the parameter name has to
be [[invite_as]] and the CLI alias is wired explicitly.

The [[EMAIL...]] positional argument has no autocompletion because
the target addresses are, by construction, not yet users of the
server; completing from the member list would be actively
misleading.

<<functions>>=
@users_app.command("invite")
def _users_invite_handler(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    emails: Annotated[
        list[str],
        typer.Argument(
            help="email addresses to invite",
        ),
    ],
    invite_as: Annotated[
        InviteRole,
        typer.Option(
            "--as",
            "-a",
            help="organisation role assigned to invitees",
        ),
    ] = InviteRole.member,
    expires_in: Annotated[
        Optional[int],
        typer.Option(
            "--expires-in",
            help="invitation expiry in minutes "
                 "(default: server policy)",
        ),
    ] = None,
    streams: Annotated[
        Optional[list[str]],
        typer.Option(
            "--stream",
            "-s",
            help="stream to subscribe invitees to (repeatable)",
            autocompletion=complete_stream_names,
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Invite one or more users to the Zulip organisation."""
    users_invite_command(
        _get_client_from_context(ctx),
        SimpleNamespace(
            emails=emails,
            invite_as=invite_as,
            expires_in=expires_in,
            streams=list(streams or []),
        ),
    )
@

Stream subscriptions require numeric IDs, not names, so we resolve
each requested stream before building the request.
Resolving up-front means an unknown stream name aborts with a clear
[[Error: Invalid channel name ...]] \emph{before} any invitation is
sent, rather than after a partial success.

The [[invitee_emails]] field accepts both comma-separated and
newline-separated strings; we prefer newlines because commas inside
display names or anti-spam quoting would otherwise have to be
escaped manually.

Omitting [[invite_expires_in_minutes]] and [[stream_ids]] from the
request when the user did not ask for them lets the server apply its
own defaults rather than forcing an empty list to mean
\enquote{subscribe to nothing}.

<<functions>>=
def users_invite_command(client, args):
    """Create invitations for the given emails."""
    stream_ids = [
        _require_success(client.get_stream_id(name))["stream_id"]
        for name in args.streams
    ]

    request = {
        "invitee_emails": "\n".join(args.emails),
        "invite_as": _INVITE_ROLE_CODES[args.invite_as.value],
    }
    if args.expires_in is not None:
        request["invite_expires_in_minutes"] = args.expires_in
    if stream_ids:
        request["stream_ids"] = stream_ids

    _require_success(client.call_endpoint(
        url="invites",
        method="POST",
        request=request,
    ))
@

