\section{The \texttt{read} subcommand}

The [[read]] command reads the complete history of a stream or one
topic within a stream.
When the caller omits the stream entirely, we fall back to an otherwise
empty narrow and let Zulip interpret that as all messages this user can
see.
That makes the bare [[zulipcli read -U]] form useful for cron jobs and
other inbox sweeps, while keeping the more specific stream/topic form for
interactive inspection.

To read a whole history rather than one page, we follow Zulip's own
[[get-history]] example: start at anchor [[0]], which means the oldest
message in the narrow, and keep advancing the anchor to the message
immediately after the most recent batch we just printed.
That yields oldest-to-newest output without collecting the whole history
in memory first.

Unread filtering is still just another Zulip narrow term, so the command
can expose it as a boolean option without changing the pagination logic.

Marking messages as read needs more care. We should only acknowledge
messages that actually reached stdout, so we collect the IDs of messages
whose printing succeeded and update their flags only after the whole read
finishes. That way an encoding failure or broken pipe cannot silently
mark unseen messages as read.

<<constants>>=
HISTORY_BATCH_SIZE = 1000
@

