\section{The term index}
\label{sec:term-index}

Search quality is bounded by query quality, and a caller composing a
query has to guess what this realm calls things.
That guess is usually wrong.
Swedish compounding and definite suffixes mean one concept surfaces as
[[kryptografi]], [[kryptografin]], and [[kryptografikursen]], while
local jargon such as an assignment label or a course code is not
guessable at all.
Asking the server does not help, because Zulip exposes no vocabulary
endpoint---only search, which is the thing we are trying to aim.

Every message we already fetch carries that vocabulary, though.
Rather than crawl for it, we record the words of every message that
passes through any reading command and answer vocabulary questions from
that local record.
The index costs no extra API calls, and it grows precisely where the
user actually works.

\subsection{Where the index lives}

Two realms must never blend vocabularies.
A term that is common at work says nothing about a hobby server, and
merging the two would corrupt both the frequency counts and the
co-occurrence signal that \cref{sec:terms} reports.
We therefore key the database on the site the client is configured for.

The client's [[base_url]] is the only realm identity available without
spending an API call, but it is a URL rather than a filename.
Stripping the scheme and any path, then replacing everything outside a
safe character set, leaves a key that is both stable and readable in a
directory listing.
The type check is not defensive padding: [[base_url]] is a plain
attribute, so a test double presents an object rather than a string
there, and an unguarded substitution would fail inside every test that
reads messages.

<<constants>>=
TERM_INDEX_UNKNOWN_SITE = "unknown"
TERM_INDEX_SCHEME_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://", re.I)
TERM_INDEX_UNSAFE_RE = re.compile(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]+")
<<functions>>=
def _term_index_site(client):
    """Return a filesystem-safe site key for `client`."""
    base_url = getattr(client, "base_url", None)
    if not isinstance(base_url, str):
        return TERM_INDEX_UNKNOWN_SITE
    site = TERM_INDEX_SCHEME_RE.sub("", base_url).split("/")[0]
    return TERM_INDEX_UNSAFE_RE.sub("_", site) or \
        TERM_INDEX_UNKNOWN_SITE
@

The index is regenerable but not disposable mid-run, which is what the
XDG base directory specification calls data rather than cache.
Honouring [[XDG_DATA_HOME]] before the default also gives tests and
sandboxes a single variable that relocates the whole thing, which is
what keeps the test suite from writing into the developer's own index.

<<functions>>=
def _term_data_dir():
    """Return the directory holding zulipcli's local data."""
    xdg_data_home = os.environ.get("XDG_DATA_HOME")
    if xdg_data_home:
        return Path(xdg_data_home) / "zulipcli"
    return Path.home() / ".local" / "share" / "zulipcli"
@

<<functions>>=
def _term_index_path(client):
    """Return the term-index database path for `client`."""
    return _term_data_dir() / f"terms-{_term_index_site(client)}.db"
@

