\subsection{The database}

Three tables answer the three questions the index exists for: which
messages have already been counted, how often each term occurs, and
which terms occur together.

Pairs are stored in both directions, which doubles the rows.
That is deliberate.
A lookup then reads [[WHERE term = ?]], which uses the leftmost column
of the composite primary key; storing each pair once would force
[[WHERE term = ? OR other = ?]], and the second alternative cannot use
that key at all.
For the same reason [[pairs]] carries no secondary index---it would
only duplicate the key's prefix---and [[WITHOUT ROWID]] stores the rows
inside the key itself instead of in a separate table.

<<constants>>=
TERM_INDEX_SCHEMA = """
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (
    message_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY
);
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS terms (
    term TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
    hits INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS pairs (
    term TEXT NOT NULL,
    other TEXT NOT NULL,
    hits INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    PRIMARY KEY (term, other)
) WITHOUT ROWID;
"""
@

The [[bot]] command keeps a process online that writes the index on
every batch it reads, so an interactive lookup can easily arrive while
a write is in flight.
Write-ahead logging is what makes that safe without either side
blocking: readers see the last committed state while a writer appends.
The timeout covers the remaining case of two writers, and
[[synchronous=NORMAL]] is the setting WAL is designed around, which
spares us an fsync for every page of history we read.

The file mode matters because the index records what its owner reads.
Nothing in it can reconstruct a message, but on a shared machine the
word list is still the user's business alone.

<<constants>>=
TERM_INDEX_TIMEOUT = 5.0
TERM_INDEX_FILE_MODE = 0o600
<<functions>>=
def _term_index_connection(client):
    """Open the term index for `client`, creating it when needed."""
    path = _term_index_path(client)
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    connection = sqlite3.connect(path, timeout=TERM_INDEX_TIMEOUT)
    connection.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL")
    connection.execute("PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL")
    connection.executescript(TERM_INDEX_SCHEMA)
    try:
        path.chmod(TERM_INDEX_FILE_MODE)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return connection
@

\subsection{What counts as a term}

A term is a run of letters and digits, optionally joined by a hyphen or
an apostrophe, casefolded.
The word pattern excludes [[_]] deliberately: Python's [[\w]] includes
it, which would fuse an identifier into a single token and let Markdown
emphasis leak into the vocabulary.
Everything else [[\w]] matches stays, and that is the point---under
Python's Unicode semantics the pattern keeps every letter, so a Swedish
realm indexes Swedish words rather than a mangled transliteration.
Casefolding rather than lowercasing matches
[[_matches_completion()]] from \cref{sec:completion}, so the whole
module agrees on one notion of caseless equality.

Digits survive inside a token, but a token containing no letter at all
is dropped: an assignment label like [[inl1]] or a course code is
exactly the vocabulary a caller cannot guess, whereas a bare year is
noise.

The order of the four substitutions is load-bearing.
A fenced code block may itself contain quoted lines and links, so code
goes first and takes its contents with it.
Mentions and channel references go last because they are the only
pattern that survives the earlier passes intact; stripping them keeps
people's names, which are not realm vocabulary, from dominating the
co-occurrence counts.

<<constants>>=
TERM_MIN_LENGTH = 3
TERM_MAX_PER_MESSAGE = 16
TERM_CODE_RE = re.compile(
    r"```.*?```|~~~.*?~~~|`[^`\n]*`",
    re.DOTALL,
)
TERM_QUOTE_RE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*>.*$", re.MULTILINE)
TERM_URL_RE = re.compile(r"(?:https?://|www\.)\S+")
TERM_MENTION_RE = re.compile(r"[@#]_?\*{1,2}[^*]*\*{1,2}")
TERM_WORD_RE = re.compile(r"[^\W_]+(?:[-'][^\W_]+)*")
@

Function words have to go.
Without them every co-occurrence list reads [[och]], [[att]], [[det]],
[[the]], [[and]]---an answer that is perfectly correct and completely
useless, and improving queries is the only reason this index exists.
The list covers Swedish and English because this realm mixes them, and
it needs only the longer words, since anything shorter than the minimum
length is dropped before the list is consulted.

We stop short of stemming, and that is a decision rather than an
omission.
Swedish compounding and definite suffixes are precisely the variation
\cref{sec:terms} exists to surface: someone who types [[kryptografi]]
wants to be told that the realm writes [[kryptografin]].
A stemmer would collapse that distinction before the similarity lookup
ever saw it, destroying the signal it feeds on.
Here the fuzzy matcher \emph{is} the stemmer.

<<constants>>=
TERM_STOPWORDS = frozenset(
    """
    about after also and any are back because been before being both
    but can could did does doing done down each else even every for
    from get gets getting give going good got had has have here how
    into its just know like made make many may maybe might more most
    much must need not now off one only other our out over own put
    really said same say see seem should since some still such take
    than that the their them then there these they thing think this
    those though thought through too try use used using very want was
    way well were what when where which while who why will with would
    yes yet you your
    alla alltid andra att bara blev bli blir borde bra både där då dem
    den denna deras dessa det detta din ditt eftersom efter eller ett
    fick finns flera från får för före gick går göra gör hade han hans
    har hela heller hen hennes hon honom hos hur här igen inga ingen
    inget inte kan kanske kom komma kommer kunde kunna lite man med
    mellan men mer mest mig min mina mitt mycket många måste något
    några någon nästa när och också oss redan sedan ser sig sin sina
    sitt ska skall skulle som står säger sätt till tycker under utan
    vad var vara varit varje vem vet vid vilka vilken vill väl vår
    våra även över
    """.split()
)
@

The per-message cap bounds the pair count.
Co-occurrence is quadratic in the terms of a single message, so one
unbounded long post could contribute thousands of pairs by itself.
Taking the first distinct terms in document order rather than the
rarest keeps this a single pass with no dependence on global counts,
and a Zulip message states its subject in its opening sentence---the
tail is usually quoted context or a sign-off.

<<functions>>=
def _message_terms(content):
    """Return the distinct indexable terms of one message body."""
    text = TERM_CODE_RE.sub(" ", content)
    text = TERM_QUOTE_RE.sub(" ", text)
    text = TERM_URL_RE.sub(" ", text)
    text = TERM_MENTION_RE.sub(" ", text)

    terms = []
    seen = set()
    for match in TERM_WORD_RE.finditer(text):
        term = match.group().casefold()
        if len(term) < TERM_MIN_LENGTH or term in seen:
            continue
        if term in TERM_STOPWORDS:
            continue
        if not any(character.isalpha() for character in term):
            continue
        seen.add(term)
        terms.append(term)
        if len(terms) == TERM_MAX_PER_MESSAGE:
            break
    return terms
@

