\section{The \texttt{search} subcommand}
\label{sec:search}

The commands so far all answer \enquote{what happened here}: point them
at a stream, a topic, or a conversation and they replay it.
[[search]] answers the prior question---\enquote{where was this
mentioned}---which is the one an agent or a newcomer actually has,
because neither knows the stream and topic yet.

Zulip does the matching; the work here is scope and shape.
The scope problem comes from the fact that a Zulip narrow is a
conjunction: every term in it must hold at once.
Two [[stream]] terms in one narrow therefore ask for messages posted in
two channels simultaneously, which is nothing at all, so a repeatable
[[--stream]] cannot become a longer narrow.
It has to become \emph{more narrows}, run separately and merged by
message ID.
\Cref{sec:reading-engine} already contains that pattern in
[[_get_all_visible_unread_messages()]], which merges an ordinary unread
query with a public-channel one; [[search]] generalises it to the cross
product of the scope options.

That same conjunction is why [[--sender]] takes one regex rather than
many.
A repeatable version would look symmetric with [[--stream]] but would
quietly return nothing whenever it was used with two patterns, and a
silent empty result is worse than a missing feature.

<<functions>>=
class SearchScope(str, Enum):
    """Conversation kinds a search can be limited to."""

    all = "all"
    streams = "streams"
    dms = "dms"
@

<<functions>>=
class SearchHas(str, Enum):
    """Message contents a search can require."""

    link = "link"
    image = "image"
    attachment = "attachment"
@

Enumerations rather than a family of mutually exclusive flags: Typer
derives both validation and completion from them, and a caller cannot
express a contradiction such as streams-only-and-DMs-only in the first
place.
This follows [[BotStdinFormat]] from \cref{sec:bot}.

The default limit exists because search is the one command whose result
set is unbounded by anything the user said.
[[read general]] is as long as that channel; [[search the]] is as long
as the realm's whole history.
Fifty recent hits fit a screen and answer the question that was asked,
and [[0]] restores the unbounded behaviour for scripts that want it.

The narrow cap guards the fan-out.
Each combination is a full paginated scan, so five streams crossed with
five topics is twenty-five sequential passes over history---slow enough
to look like a hang, and almost never what the caller meant.

<<constants>>=
SEARCH_DEFAULT_LIMIT = 50
SEARCH_MAX_NARROWS = 12
SEARCH_SNIPPET_WIDTH = 100
SEARCH_SNIPPET_ELLIPSIS = "..."
@

\subsection{Resolving the sender}

Sender narrows take a user ID, but making the caller look one up would
undo the point of the command.
We therefore accept the same regex that [[send --to]] and [[dm]] accept
and resolve it through the shared matcher of
[[_resolve_direct_recipients()]].

Ambiguity is an error rather than a prompt.
[[send]] can afford to ask which recipients were meant because the
answer changes who receives a message; here the caller is composing a
query, and listing every match tells them how to make it precise in one
step.

<<functions>>=
def _search_sender_id(client, pattern):
    """Return the user ID of the one sender matching `pattern`."""
    users = _resolve_direct_recipients(client, [pattern])
    described = _describe_patterns([pattern])
    if not users:
        _exit_with_error(
            f"--sender {described} matched no active users"
        )
    if len(users) > 1:
        names = ", ".join(user["full_name"] for user in users)
        _exit_with_error(
            f"--sender {described} matched several users: {names}"
        )
    return users[0]["user_id"]
@

<<test functions>>=
