\subsection{Building the queries}

The terms split cleanly by what they depend on.
One group is fixed for the whole invocation---the words themselves, the
sender, the conversation kind, the content requirements, and unread
filtering---and can be computed once.

<<functions>>=
def _search_base_terms(client, args):
    """Return the narrow terms shared by every search query."""
    terms = [
        {"operator": "search", "operand": word}
        for word in args.query
    ]
    if args.sender:
        terms.append({
            "operator": "sender",
            "operand": _search_sender_id(client, args.sender),
        })
    if args.scope == SearchScope.dms:
        terms.append({"operator": "is", "operand": "dm"})
    elif args.scope == SearchScope.streams:
        terms.append({
            "operator": "is",
            "operand": "dm",
            "negated": True,
        })
    for required in args.has or []:
        terms.append({"operator": "has", "operand": required.value})
    if args.unread:
        terms.append({"operator": "is", "operand": "unread"})
    return terms
@

Each positional argument becomes its own [[search]] term rather than
being joined into one string.
Zulip ANDs the terms, so the effect is the same conjunction a user
expects from a search box---but the caller's own quoting survives, and
[[search "gamla tentor" krypto]] stays a phrase plus a word instead of
collapsing into one long phrase that matches nothing.

The other group is the conversation filter, and this is where one query
becomes several.
Repeated streams and topics multiply out, and a bare topic with no
stream is left as a topic-only narrow, which Zulip accepts and which is
useful when the same topic name recurs across channels.

<<functions>>=
def _search_conversation_terms(args):
    """Return one term list per stream and topic combination."""
    streams = list(args.stream or [])
    topics = list(args.topic or [])
    if not streams and not topics:
        return [[]]

    combinations = []
    for stream in streams or [None]:
        for topic in topics or [None]:
            terms = []
            if stream is not None:
                terms.append(
                    {"operator": "stream", "operand": stream}
                )
            if topic is not None:
                terms.append(
                    {"operator": "topic", "operand": topic}
                )
            combinations.append(terms)
    return combinations
@

The last axis is how much history to look at.
By default a search covers what the account subscribes to, which is
what \enquote{search my Zulip} means to a person.
\Cref{sec:reading-engine} needs the wider public-channel scope for a
different reason---the bot has to match its event queue---and paying
for it on every search would double the queries to answer a question
nobody asked.
So the wider scope is opt-in, and it is skipped entirely under
[[--scope dms]], where a channel clause could not match anything.

<<functions>>=
def _search_narrows(client, args):
    """Return every narrow this search has to run."""
    base = _search_base_terms(client, args)
    conversations = _search_conversation_terms(args)

    scopes = [[]]
    if args.public and args.scope != SearchScope.dms:
        scopes.append([{"operator": "channels", "operand": "public"}])

    narrows = [
        scope + conversation + base
        for scope in scopes
        for conversation in conversations
    ]
    if len(narrows) > SEARCH_MAX_NARROWS:
        _exit_with_error(
            f"search would need {len(narrows)} queries; use at most "
            f"{SEARCH_MAX_NARROWS} --stream and --topic combinations"
        )
    return narrows
@

<<test functions>>=
