\subsection{Reading the newest matches first}

\Cref{sec:reading-engine} pages forward from the oldest message,
because a transcript is read in order.
A capped search wants the opposite end: the fifty most recent mentions,
not the fifty oldest, which on any established realm would be years
stale.

Zulip's anchor model is symmetric, so this is the same loop reflected.
We anchor at the newest message, request messages before it, and walk
backwards until the server reports the oldest or we have enough.
Each page is prepended, which keeps the accumulated list in
oldest-to-newest order and lets the final slice take the newest
[[limit]] entries.
The empty-page check is what guarantees termination if a server ever
returns no messages without also reporting [[found_oldest]].

<<functions>>=
def _get_newest_messages_for_narrow(client, narrow, limit):
    """Return the newest `limit` messages matching `narrow`."""
    request = _history_request(
        narrow,
        anchor="newest",
        num_before=min(limit, HISTORY_BATCH_SIZE),
        num_after=0,
    )
    messages = []
    while len(messages) < limit:
        result = _fetch_history_page(client, request)
        page = result["messages"]
        messages = page + messages
        if result["found_oldest"] or not page:
            break
        request["anchor"] = messages[0]["id"] - 1
        request["num_before"] = min(
            limit - len(messages),
            HISTORY_BATCH_SIZE,
        )
    return messages[-limit:]
@

Fetching the newest [[limit]] messages per narrow and then keeping the
newest [[limit]] of the merged result is exact, not an approximation:
any message in the union's newest [[limit]] is also within its own
narrow's newest [[limit]], so nothing that belongs in the answer can be
discarded by the per-narrow cap.

\subsection{Showing the hits}

A hit is a location, not a transcript.
The caller wants to see enough to judge relevance and enough to go
read the real thing, so the compact line carries when, who, where, the
message ID, and a flattened excerpt.

Tab-separated even on a terminal, unlike the tables used for
[[users list]] and [[streams]].
Those have bounded columns; a message excerpt does not, and a Rich
table would wrap it across lines, which destroys both the one-hit-per-
line property and the ability to pipe the output into [[grep]].
The location renders as the same [[#channel>topic]] string that Zulip's
own search box accepts, so a hit can be pasted straight back into the
web client.

<<functions>>=
def _search_message_location(message):
    """Return where one message was posted."""
    if message.get("type") == "private":
        return _read_message_target(message)
    stream = message.get("display_recipient") or ""
    return f"#{stream}>{_read_message_target(message)}"
@

<<functions>>=
def _search_snippet(content):
    """Return a one-line excerpt of a message body."""
    text = " ".join(content.split())
    if len(text) <= SEARCH_SNIPPET_WIDTH:
        return text
    trimmed = text[:SEARCH_SNIPPET_WIDTH].rstrip()
    return trimmed + SEARCH_SNIPPET_ELLIPSIS
@

Collapsing all whitespace is what makes the line format trustworthy.
Zulip message bodies contain newlines, and a body pasted verbatim would
turn one hit into several apparent rows.

<<functions>>=
def _format_search_hit(message):
    """Return one search result as a single tab-separated line."""
    return "\t".join([
        _format_message_timestamp(message),
        message["sender_full_name"],
        _search_message_location(message),
        str(message["id"]),
        _search_snippet(message.get("content") or ""),
    ])
@

\subsection{Running the search}

The command merges before it prints, which is why it does not simply
call [[_read_messages()]].
That helper fuses paging, printing, and acknowledgement into one pass,
and all three assume a single narrow; a search has to see every
narrow's results before it can tell which are the newest matches
overall, or deduplicate a message that two narrows both returned.
It still reuses the same pagination and the same renderer, so
[[--full]] output is byte-for-byte what [[read]] would have printed.

There is deliberately no [[--mark-as-read]].
With a limit in play, a search fetches more than it shows, so marking
would acknowledge messages that were truncated away; and without
[[--full]] the caller has seen an excerpt rather than the message.
Keeping the flag off also means a search can never starve the [[bot]]
worker of the unread messages it triggers on.

<<functions>>=
def search_command(client, args):
    """Search message history and print matching messages."""
    if not args.query:
        _exit_with_error("search requires a query")

    messages_by_id = {}
    for narrow in _search_narrows(client, args):
        if args.limit:
            found = _get_newest_messages_for_narrow(
                client,
                narrow,
                args.limit,
            )
        else:
            found = _get_messages_for_narrow(client, narrow)
        for message in found:
            messages_by_id[message["id"]] = message

    messages = [
        messages_by_id[message_id]
        for message_id in sorted(messages_by_id)
    ]
    if args.limit:
        messages = messages[-args.limit:]

    for message in messages:
        if args.full:
            _print_read_message(message)
        else:
            print(_format_search_hit(message))
@

<<functions>>=
def _search_handler(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    query: Annotated[
        Optional[list[str]],
        typer.Argument(
            help="word or phrase to find; repeat to require several",
