\subsection{Reporting}

Co-occurrence is reported for the single best-matching term, and the
header names it.
Summing the neighbours of every similar term would merge unrelated
subjects---a query for [[krypto]] would blend the cryptography course
with anything about cryptocurrency---and produce a list that describes
neither.

A bare invocation lists the most frequent terms instead.
That is the cold-start case: an agent that has just arrived knows no
words at all, and a frequency list is the shortest description of what
this realm talks about.

The empty-index message goes to stderr, and the command still succeeds.
Stdout stays a clean stream of data rows, so a caller can pipe [[terms]]
into another tool without having to strip an advisory line, and an
empty vocabulary is a normal state rather than a failure.

<<functions>>=
def _print_term_rows(rows):
    """Print term and count pairs as tab-separated lines."""
    for term, hits in rows:
        print(f"{term}\t{hits}")
@

<<functions>>=
def terms_command(client, args):
    """Report the vocabulary recorded in the local term index."""
    vocabulary = _term_index_vocabulary(client, args.min_count)
    if not vocabulary:
        print(TERM_INDEX_EMPTY_HINT, file=sys.stderr)
        return

    if not args.query:
        _print_term_rows(vocabulary[:args.limit])
        return

    similar = _rank_similar_terms(
        vocabulary,
        args.query.casefold(),
        args.limit,
    )
    if not similar:
        print(
            f"No indexed term resembles {args.query!r}.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return

    print("similar:")
    _print_term_rows(similar)

    best = similar[0][0]
    cooccurring = _term_index_cooccurrences(
        client,
        best,
        args.min_count,
        args.limit,
    )
    if cooccurring:
        print()
        print(f"co-occurring with {best}:")
        _print_term_rows(cooccurring)
@

