\section{The \texttt{bot} subcommand}
\label{sec:bot}

The old cron-driven pattern reran a helper on a timer and let that
helper ask Zulip whether anything unread had appeared.
That is simple, but it wakes an expensive worker even when nothing has
changed.
The [[bot]] command keeps one process online instead: it maintains a
local set of unread message IDs and only launches the worker when that
set is non-empty.

The local unread set matters because a message event is only evidence
that the message was new at one point.
By the time the worker exits, that same message may already have been
consumed by [[zulipcli read -U --mark-as-read]] or deleted.
We therefore watch every event that changes unreadness for the current
account, reconcile them into one set, and decide whether to rerun only
after draining the queue.

The worker command belongs to some other program, not to [[zulipcli]].
We therefore configure Click to stop parsing after [[bot]] and pass the
remaining arguments through untouched.
That keeps worker flags available without forcing this command to know
anything about them.

This pass-through design is invisible in the generated usage line: with
no declared positional argument, Typer can only print
[[bot [OPTIONS]]] and never mentions the worker command at all.
We therefore carry the [[--]] contract in the help text instead---the
docstring above explains it in prose, and a command [[epilog]] shows a
runnable example so a reader can copy the shape without guessing.
We keep that example on a single line: Typer renders help through Rich,
which ignores Click's [[\b]] no-rewrap marker, so a multi-line example
would be reflowed into one run-on line with a stray continuation
backslash.

The worker also needs the unread messages themselves.
Some workers are easiest to drive with the plain text that [[read]]
already prints, while others want a machine-readable format.
We therefore expose a small enum for the worker's stdin format: the
default [[read]] format and an optional [[json]] payload.

<<constants>>=
BOT_CONTEXT_SETTINGS = {
    "allow_extra_args": True,
    "ignore_unknown_options": True,
}
BOT_HELP_EPILOG = (
    "Example: zulipcli bot -- opencode run -s ses_ABC "
    '-m github-copilot/gpt-5.4 "There are new messages."'
)
BOT_EVENT_TYPES = [
    "message",
    "update_message_flags",
    "delete_message",
    "restart",
]
BOT_INITIAL_RETRY_SECONDS = 60
BOT_MAX_RETRY_SECONDS = 60 * 60
@

<<functions>>=
class BotStdinFormat(str, Enum):
    """Formats available for bot worker stdin."""

    read = "read"
    json = "json"
@

<<functions>>=
def _bot_handler(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    stdin_format: Annotated[
        BotStdinFormat,
        typer.Option(
            "--stdin-format",
            help="format for unread messages written to worker stdin",
        ),
    ] = BotStdinFormat.read,
):
    """Run an external worker whenever unread messages are pending.

    Give the worker command and all of its own arguments after a `--`
    separator; zulipcli passes them through untouched, so the worker may
    take any flags it likes. The worker is launched each time unread
    messages appear, and those unread messages are written to its stdin
    in the format chosen by `--stdin-format` (the same plain text that
    `zulipcli read` prints, or `json`).
    """
    command = list(ctx.args)
    if not command:
        _exit_with_error("bot requires a worker command")
    bot_command(
        _get_client_from_context(ctx),
        SimpleNamespace(command=command, stdin_format=stdin_format),
    )
@

