    cli._fetch_history_page(client, cli._history_request([]))

    assert indexed == [{"id": 1, "content": "migration"}]
@

The register snapshot is capped at Zulip's most recent unread messages.
When the server tells us older unread messages are missing, a lower-bound
snapshot is not good enough: the bot would make rerun decisions on stale
or incomplete data.
In that case we page through unread history ourselves.
One query covers the account's personal history, while another covers
shared public-channel history.
Merging those results keeps the bot's fallback aligned with the live
queue's [[all_public_streams]] scope.

<<functions>>=
def _get_messages_for_narrow(client, narrow):
    """Return all messages matching `narrow` from oldest to newest."""
    request = _history_request(narrow)
    messages = []
    found_newest = False
    while not found_newest:
        result = _fetch_history_page(client, request)
        found_newest = result["found_newest"]
        if result["messages"]:
            messages.extend(result["messages"])
            request["anchor"] = result["messages"][-1]["id"] + 1
    return messages
@

Zulip's plain [[is:unread]] search only covers the account's personal
history.
Shared public-channel history needs an explicit [[channels public]]
clause.
We therefore merge both unread searches, deduplicate by message ID, and
sort by ID so callers see one oldest-to-newest stream of visible unread
messages.

<<functions>>=
def _get_all_visible_unread_messages(client):
    """Return all visible unread messages from oldest to newest."""
    messages_by_id = {}
    for narrow in (
        [{"operator": "is", "operand": "unread"}],
        [
            {"operator": "channels", "operand": "public"},
            {"operator": "is", "operand": "unread"},
        ],
    ):
        for message in _get_messages_for_narrow(client, narrow):
            messages_by_id[message["id"]] = message
    return [
        messages_by_id[message_id]
        for message_id in sorted(messages_by_id)
    ]
@

<<functions>>=
def _get_all_unread_message_ids(client):
    """Return the IDs of all visible unread messages."""
    return {
        message["id"]
        for message in _get_all_visible_unread_messages(client)
    }
@

The queue registration helper combines those two pieces: use the cheap
snapshot when it is complete, and fall back to the exact unread scan
only when the server reports truncation.
It also asks Zulip for message events from all public streams, because
the bot's scope is every message visible to the account, not just
streams the account is subscribed to.

<<functions>>=
def _register_bot_queue(client):
    """Register the bot queue and seed its unread state."""
    result = _require_success(client.register(
        event_types=BOT_EVENT_TYPES,
        all_public_streams=True,
        client_gravatar=False,
    ))
    unread_msgs = result.get("unread_msgs", {})
    pending_unread_ids = _unread_message_ids_from_snapshot(unread_msgs)
    if unread_msgs.get("old_unreads_missing", False):
        pending_unread_ids = _get_all_unread_message_ids(client)
    return result["queue_id"], result["last_event_id"], pending_unread_ids
@

The queue tells us whether unread work exists, but the worker needs the
current messages themselves.
Fetching a fresh visible-unread snapshot immediately before launch keeps
the worker's stdin aligned with reality: if every queued message was
already consumed or deleted, we can skip the spawn entirely.

<<functions>>=
def _bot_messages_to_stdin(messages, stdin_format):
    """Return the worker stdin payload for `messages`."""
    if stdin_format == BotStdinFormat.json:
        return json.dumps(messages)
    buffer = io.StringIO()
    for message in messages:
        _write_read_message(buffer, message)
    return buffer.getvalue()
@

<<functions>>=
def _get_bot_worker_messages(client):
    """Return visible unread messages for the next bot worker run."""
    return _get_all_visible_unread_messages(client)
@

