Spawning the worker is the only place where the bot touches the local
process table.
Treating startup failures as ordinary command-line errors gives the user
an immediate, actionable message instead of leaving the bot in a useless
retry loop.

<<functions>>=
def _run_bot_worker(command, stdin_payload):
    """Run the configured worker command."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(command, input=stdin_payload, text=True)
    except OSError as error:
        _exit_with_error(
            f"could not start worker command {command[0]!r}: {error}"
        )
@

Queue deletion is only best-effort.
If cleanup itself fails, Zulip will still garbage-collect the queue after
its idle timeout.

<<functions>>=
def _discard_queue(client, queue_id):
    """Best-effort deregistration of a bot queue."""
    if queue_id:
        try:
            client.deregister(queue_id)
        except Exception:
            pass
@

The main loop is now straightforward to describe:
wait until the unread set becomes non-empty, run the worker exactly once,
drain the queue to reconcile everything that happened during that run,
and only then decide whether another run is still justified.
Non-zero worker exits keep the bot alive, but only after the requested
backoff and only while unread work still remains.

Right before each launch, we replace the queue-derived unread-ID set with
the current visible unread messages and serialize those messages for the
worker's stdin.
That extra fetch costs one more request per run, but it avoids spawning a
worker for unread IDs that already vanished and makes the worker's input
precise.

<<functions>>=
def bot_command(client, args):
    """Run a worker whenever visible unread messages are pending."""
    queue_id = None
    last_event_id = None
    pending_unread_ids = set()
    failed_runs = 0
    try:
        <<register the initial bot queue>>
        while True:
            <<wait for unread work>>
            <<run the worker once>>
            <<reconcile unread state after the worker>>
            <<back off before retrying failed work>>
    finally:
        _discard_queue(client, queue_id)
@

The very first register call seeds both the queue cursor and the unread
set.
That means the bot reacts immediately if unread messages already exist
when it starts.

<<register the initial bot queue>>=
queue_id, last_event_id, pending_unread_ids = _register_bot_queue(client)
@

An idle bot blocks in [[get_events()]] until something interesting
happens.
That keeps the process quiet while still letting Zulip wake it as soon as
new unread work appears.

<<wait for unread work>>=
while not pending_unread_ids:
    queue_id, last_event_id, pending_unread_ids = _refresh_bot_queue(
        client,
        queue_id,
        last_event_id,
        pending_unread_ids,
        dont_block=False,
    )
@

Worker execution itself is synchronous.
That is deliberate: one active worker is enough, because the queue lets
us merge every event that arrived while it was busy into one follow-up
decision.
We keep track of messages that were deleted or explicitly restored to
unread during that run, because those newer queue events override the
original stdin snapshot when we decide what can be acknowledged.

<<run the worker once>>=
messages = _get_bot_worker_messages(client)
pending_unread_ids = {message["id"] for message in messages}
if not pending_unread_ids:
    failed_runs = 0
    continue
deleted_message_ids = set()
restored_unread_ids = set()
worker_result = _run_bot_worker(
    args.command,
    _bot_messages_to_stdin(messages, args.stdin_format),
)
@

Draining the queue immediately after the worker run is what prevents a
stale message event from forcing a second, empty worker invocation.
If the worker succeeded, we also treat its stdin batch as acknowledged
work.
The worker may already have marked those messages as read itself, so we
first drain the queue and only acknowledge the subset of that batch that
was neither deleted nor explicitly marked unread while the worker was
busy.
If the worker succeeded, or if no unread work remains after the drain, we
reset the failure counter and return to the top of the loop.

<<reconcile unread state after the worker>>=
queue_id, last_event_id, pending_unread_ids = _refresh_bot_queue(
    client,
    queue_id,
    last_event_id,
    pending_unread_ids,
    dont_block=True,
    deleted_message_ids=deleted_message_ids,
    restored_unread_ids=restored_unread_ids,
)
if worker_result.returncode == 0:
    message_ids_to_mark = [
        message["id"]
        for message in messages
        if message["id"] not in deleted_message_ids
        and message["id"] not in restored_unread_ids
    ]
    _mark_message_ids_as_read(client, message_ids_to_mark)
    pending_unread_ids.difference_update(message_ids_to_mark)
if worker_result.returncode == 0 or not pending_unread_ids:
    failed_runs = 0
    continue
@

Retry sleeps can easily outlast the current queue's idle lifetime.
We therefore resynchronize once the backoff expires before trusting the
old unread set enough to rerun the worker again.

<<back off before retrying failed work>>=
failed_runs += 1
delay_seconds = _bot_retry_delay_seconds(failed_runs)
print(
    "Error: worker exited with status "
    f"{worker_result.returncode}; unread messages remain; "
    f"retrying in {delay_seconds // 60}m",
    file=sys.stderr,
)
time.sleep(delay_seconds)
queue_id, last_event_id, pending_unread_ids = _refresh_bot_queue(
    client,
    queue_id,
    last_event_id,
    pending_unread_ids,
    dont_block=True,
)
if not pending_unread_ids:
    failed_runs = 0
